[Terminal runner-phase tournament classes]
  vertices: the thirteen speeds in increasing-speed order
  observable: residues s mod 14 at the denominator-14 apex
  switch/gauge: half-clock cutoff; collisions and diameters broken by speed order
  tie Hamiltonian path: increasing speed
  quotient preserves: apex winding/tie class
  quotient destroys: q-threshold divisibility and off-apex escape size

  AP                       class=T0 q=14, M=1/14, denoms=[14] score_hist=((5, 6), (6, 1), (7, 6)) c3=85 scc=(13,) hp=2641713
  GW 12->24                class=T1 q=14, M=1/14, denoms=[14] score_hist=((4, 2), (5, 4), (6, 1), (7, 4), (8, 2)) c3=79 scc=(13,) hp=1780425
  loose 8->16              class=T2 q=14, M=2/23, denoms=[23] score_hist=((5, 2), (6, 9), (7, 2)) c3=89 scc=(13,) hp=3415811
  loose 10->20             class=T3 q=14, M=2/27, denoms=[27] score_hist=((4, 2), (5, 4), (6, 1), (7, 4), (8, 2)) c3=79 scc=(13,) hp=1795641
  residue-liar 12->26      class=T0 q=12, M=1/12, denoms=[12] score_hist=((5, 6), (6, 1), (7, 6)) c3=85 scc=(13,) hp=2641713
  Farey near-miss 12->36   class=T4 q=14, M=3/41, denoms=[41] score_hist=((4, 4), (5, 2), (6, 1), (7, 2), (8, 4)) c3=73 scc=(13,) hp=1236275
  class groups:
    T0: AP, residue-liar 12->26
    T1: GW 12->24
    T2: loose 8->16
    T3: loose 10->20
    T4: Farey near-miss 12->36

[Proof readout]
  Tournament Analysis is useful only after three exact choices are fixed:
    (1) vertex set, (2) pairwise observable/cutoff, (3) tie Hamiltonian path.
  For the apex clock, the achieved tournament classes are a small finite
  atlas inside the 2x7 clock.  For terminal AP/GW rows, the apex-winding
  quotient separates some geometry but not the full LRC statement.
  The next summit lemma should be a state lift:
    bad LRC14 row -> achieved tournament/OCF class -> forbidden class absent,
  with q-threshold and off-apex witness data carried outside the quotient.
